    "conventions": "Singapore's communities name people differently. Chinese Singaporeans put the family name first (Tan Wei Ming), usually spelled in the dialect of their grandparents (Tan, Lim, Ng, Goh are Hokkien or Teochew spellings), and most also carry an English name used in front (Jason Tan Wei Ming, known as Jason Tan). Malay names have no family name: the second part is the father's name after bin (son of) or binte (daughter of), so Nurul Huda binte Ahmad is usually just Nurul. Many Tamil Singaporeans write their father's name after their own, sometimes with s/o or d/o (son of, daughter of). Eurasians carry Portuguese, Dutch or British family names. The game shows a short everyday form: English or Malay or Tamil given name plus family or father's name; Chinese names keep family first when no English name is used.",
